# Vulnerabilities waived for the container image scan in .github/workflows/release.yml.
#
# **This file is empty on purpose, and it went back to empty on 2026-08-17 (#492).**
#
# The gate blocks a release on CRITICAL and HIGH findings **that have a fix available**; unfixed ones
# are already excluded by `--ignore-unfixed` and do not belong here. So an entry in this file means
# something narrower and more deliberate: *a fix exists and we are shipping without it anyway*.
#
# Every entry needs a reason and a date on the line above it, and the reason has to say why the fix
# cannot be taken rather than that it is inconvenient — a waiver with no expiry is a gate that has
# been quietly switched off. The usual right answer instead is to bump the base image digest in the
# Dockerfile, which is one line and takes effect on the next build.
#
# Format: one CVE id per line, `#` comments. Trivy reads this file from the repository root.
#
# Example of the shape an entry must take:
#
#   # 2026-08-12 — CVE-2026-00000 in the JRE base layer. Fixed in temurin 25.0.4, which regresses
#   # <thing>; revisit when 25.0.5 ships. Not reachable from our code path: <why>.
#   CVE-2026-00000
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE LAST EIGHT ENTRIES TAUGHT, KEPT BECAUSE IT WILL HAPPEN AGAIN
#
# Between 2026-08-14 and 2026-08-17 this file held two, then eight, waivers — all for the Go
# standard library compiled into `/usr/bin/pebble`, a service manager carried in Temurin's
# Ubuntu-based JRE and never executed here. Each batch was individually justified: the finding was
# genuinely unreachable, the base-image bump was genuinely tried first, the reason and the date were
# genuinely written down. The policy above was followed exactly, every time.
#
# It was still the wrong outcome. pebble's Go toolchain is frozen until Temurin rebuilds, so the
# list grew on the Go release cadence rather than on anything this project did — a subscription to a
# CVE feed rather than a waiver. #492 changed the base image instead (ADR-017) and all eight
# entries were deleted in the same change.
#
# **So: if a second entry ever appears here for the same root cause as the first, that is the
# signal.** Not the tenth, not when it gets annoying. Two entries sharing a cause means the cause is
# structural, and the question stops being "is this one reachable?" and becomes "why are we still
# shipping the thing that generates them?". Re-making a true argument on a schedule someone else
# sets is how a gate stops being read.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 2026-08-26 — CVE-2026-14456, an unbounded-allocation DoS in OpenSSL's QUIC *server* listener.
# 2026-08-31 — **half of this entry is now gone.** nginx-unprivileged:1.31-alpine rebuilt onto
# openssl 3.5.8-r0 (digest d9083fe4, Alpine 3.24.1) and the frontend Dockerfile is pinned to it, so
# the frontend image no longer carries the finding — `trivy image ... --ignorefile /dev/null` returns
# 0 HIGH/CRITICAL on d9083fe4 and 2 on the 901e944d it replaced. What is left is the JRE half:
# liberica-openjre-alpine:25 still has not rebuilt (its current digest is byte-identical to the one
# pinned in events-bff/ and events-importer/) and still ships libssl3/libcrypto3 3.5.7-r0.
# Not reachable in the two JRE images: the only binaries linking libssl.so.3 are BusyBox's
# ssl_client and apk, neither of which runs. (The frontend argument, kept because the pin can move
# back: it declares a single `listen 8080;` with no ssl, quic or http3 directive, so nginx never
# creates a QUIC listener.)
# Delete on the next rebuild of the Liberica base — this clears itself, unlike the pebble entries
# above. Tracked by #770, which is what stops it becoming permanent.
CVE-2026-14456
